Because of those terms, the SOURCE REPOSITORY ships no game data (no ATET.DAT /
saba.dat, no extracted audio or music) and no original program code, with two
exceptions: app_icon.ico, which is built into the Windows executable, is a
converted copy of one of the game's mood-face graphics (tile 0 of data chunk 6),
and a few short texts and tables (menu strings, the credits pages, the default
high-score table) are transcribed from the original program. The port decodes
everything else from a copy of the game you supply, at runtime (see README.md);
nothing in the original data file is modified.

SABA (Super ACiD Block Attack)
------------------------------
In 2002 the authors re-released the same game, renamed SABA, to avoid confusion
with The Tetris Company's products. SABA is (C) 1997/2002 Jason Pimble & Scott
Emerle (same credits as above) and is distributed by them as freeware. Its data
file `saba.dat` is byte-identical to `ATET.DAT` except the title-screen logo, so
this port plays the same game from either.

RELEASE downloads contain no game data by default. SABA's own notice allows
distribution only as the original self-extracting archive and asks anyone who
includes it in a collection to contact the authors, so the release workflow
bundles SABA's intact, unmodified archive `saba.zip` only when the maintainer has
that permission on record (its BUNDLE_SABA switch). The port itself stays
asset-free and reads `saba.dat` from such an archive at runtime. You can always
drop in ACiD Tetris's own `ATET.DAT` instead (see README.md).

